# 🛡️ Day 2 — DNS & Web Basics

## 📚 Topics Learned

* **DNS (Domain Name System):** Converts domain names like `google.com` into IP addresses.
* **IP Address:** A network address used to identify a destination on a network.
* **Web Server:** A computer/system that receives requests and sends website data back.
* **HTTP:** A protocol used for communication between a browser and a web server.
* **HTTPS:** The secure version of HTTP, using encryption.
* **URL:** The address used to access a resource on the web.
* **curl:** A command-line tool used to communicate with web servers and view their responses.

## 🧪 Commands Practiced

```bash
nslookup google.com
ping google.com
curl https://google.com
```

## 🔍 What I Practiced

* Used `nslookup` to find IP addresses associated with `google.com`.
* Learned that one domain can have multiple IP addresses.
* Used `ping` to see which IP address `google.com` resolved to.
* Used `curl` to request a webpage directly from the terminal.
* Learned that the response from a web server can contain HTML/webpage data.

## 🧠 Key Takeaways

> DNS helps translate domain names into IP addresses.

> One domain can have multiple IP addresses because large services can use multiple servers and infrastructure.

> A browser sends requests to web servers, and web servers send responses back.

> `curl` lets me communicate with a web server from the terminal without using a graphical browser.



